 \section{Method}
 
 \subsection{Pipeline Overview}
 
-Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} summarizes the six-stage pipeline. The full pipeline proceeds through six stages: (1)~component detection via oriented bounding box (OBB) YOLO model; (2)~component occlusion (filling detected regions with local median color); (3)~Sauvola adaptive binarization; (4)~connected component labeling with morphological post-processing; (5)~wire endpoint extraction via principal component analysis (PCA) on endpoint regions; and (6)~wire joining via the endpoint-graph model with degree-budget completion. The output is a netlist mapping component pins to electrical nodes, suitable for SPICE netlist generation. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline_examples} shows two representative circuits at each stage.
-
-\subsection{Component Detection}
-\label{sec:component_detection}
-
-Stage~1 is a YOLO26m oriented bounding box (OBB) detector fine-tuned on CGHD-1152~\cite{bayer2021cghd,cghd1152data}. Hand-drawn parts sit at arbitrary angles, and an axis-aligned box around a diagonal resistor also encloses the wire segments entering it, which the occlusion stage would then erase along with the component. The detector therefore predicts orientation.
-
-We reduce CGHD's 61 component classes to 16. Eight targets absorb variants the netlist does not distinguish. A Zener, light-emitting, or thyrector diode is a \texttt{diode} for the purpose of topology extraction (Table~\ref{tab:class_merge}). Seven classes pass through unchanged: \texttt{terminal}, \texttt{crossover}, \texttt{switch}, \texttt{text}, \texttt{junction}, \texttt{gnd}, and \texttt{vss}. A catch-all \texttt{other} absorbs the remaining 30 rare classes (transformer, thyristor, triac, relay, optocoupler, and similar), which the detector localizes but does not discriminate; these devices fall outside the simulatable set and are excluded from the net-level benchmark in any case. The join model consumes component \emph{geometry} and pin locations, not device identity, so class granularity finer than the netlist emits gives the detector further classes to separate while no downstream stage reads the result.
-
-Training excludes drafter\_0, whose drawing style diverges markedly from the remaining drafters, leaving a random 85/15 split of 2652 training and 468 validation images. We fine-tune at 1024\,px for 200 epochs with AdamW ($\text{lr}_0 = 10^{-3}$, cosine decay), batch~17, seed~42, under mosaic, mixup, RandAugment, and moderate affine and HSV jitter. On the held-out validation split the released checkpoint attains mAP@0.5~=~89.0\%, mAP@0.5:0.95~=~78.5\%, precision~=~95.8\%, and recall~=~88.6\%. Per-class recall ranges from 100\% (\texttt{operational\_amplifier}) down to 70.7\% (\texttt{crossover}), which trails the next lowest class (\texttt{vss}, 83.1\%) by 12.4 points: two wires that cross without a hop marker are locally indistinguishable from an ordinary junction-free crossing. Training logs and the full 61-to-16 class map are released with the code.
